## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design pluggable runtime extensions, handle concurrency considerations, and understand trade-offs between in-process versus out-of-process sinks.

## Key Concepts

- Plugin registry and dynamic loading at runtime
- Minimal interface contracts for telemetry sinks
- Backpressure, batching, and failure isolation
- Stateless versus stateful sinks and lifecycle management

## Code Example

```javascript
interface TelemetrySink {
  send(event: TelemetryEvent): Promise<void>;
  flush?(): Promise<void>;
  shutdown?(): Promise<void>;
}

class PluginRegistry {
  #sinks = new Map();

  register(name, factory) {
    this.#sinks.set(name, factory(this));
  }

  get(name) {
    return this.#sinks.get(name);
  }

  list() {
    return Array.from(this.#sinks.keys());
  }
}

class SyslogSink {
  #buffer = [];
  #batchSize = 100;
  #flushInterval = 5000;
  #paused = false;
  #controller = new AbortController();

  constructor(options = {}) {
    this.#batchSize = options.batchSize ?? 100;
    this.#flushInterval = options.flushInterval ?? 5000;
    this.#startFlushTimer();
  }

  async send(event) {
    if (this.#paused) {
      throw new Error('Backpressure: sink is paused');
    }

    this.#buffer.push(event);

    if (this.#buffer.length >= this.#batchSize) {
      await this.flush();
    }

    if (this.#buffer.length > this.#batchSize * 0.8) {
      this.#paused = true;
    }
  }

  async flush() {
    if (this.#buffer.length === 0) return;

    const batch = this.#buffer.splice(0, this.#batchSize);
    try {
      await this.#sendToSyslog(batch);
      this.#paused = false;
    } catch (err) {
      this.#buffer.unshift(...batch);
      throw err;
    }
  }

  async shutdown() {
    this.#controller.abort();
    await this.flush();
  }

  #startFlushTimer() {
    setInterval(() => this.flush(), this.#flushInterval);
  }

  async #sendToSyslog(batch) {
    // Send batch to syslog endpoint
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new PluginRegistry();
registry.register('syslog', () => new SyslogSink({ batchSize: 50 }));
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of the Command pattern, undo/redo implementation, and state management in a minimal system.

## Key Concepts

- Command pattern with execute/undo methods
- Receiver (TextEditor) and Command objects
- History stack for undo/redo operations
- State capture for reversible operations
- Edge cases: boundary checking, no-op commands

## Code Example

```javascript
class Editor {
  constructor() { this.text = ''; }

  insert(pos, ch) {
    this.text = this.text.slice(0, pos) + ch + this.text.slice(pos);
  }

  delete(pos, len) {
    const removed = this.text.slice(pos, pos + len);
    this.text = this.text.slice(0, pos) + this.text.slice(pos + len);
    return removed;
  }
}

class InsertCommand {
  constructor(editor, pos, ch) {
    this.editor = editor;
    this.pos = pos;
    this.ch = ch;
  }

  execute() {
    this.editor.insert(this.pos, this.ch);
  }

  undo() {
    this.editor.delete(this.pos, 1);
  }
}

class History {
  constructor() {
    this.undoStack = [];
    this.redoStack = [];
  }

  execute(command) {
    command.execute();
    this.undoStack.push(command);
    this.redoStack = [];
  }

  undo() {
    if (this.undoStack.length === 0) return;
    const command = this.undoStack.pop();
    command.undo();
    this.redoStack.push(command);
  }
}

// Example: Insert 'A' at position 0, then undo
const editor = new Editor();
const history = new History();
const insertA = new InsertCommand(editor, 0, 'A');

history.execute(insertA); // text = 'A'
history.undo(); // text = ''
```

## Implementation Notes

- Commands capture all state needed for undo
- History maintains separate undo/redo stacks
- Each operation is atomic and reversible
- Position validation ensures safe operations

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design pluggable, runtime-extendable components with clean isolation between tenants and core logic. It exercises registry design, immutable pipelines, and safe runtime extension.

## Key Concepts
- Chain of Responsibility
- Registry + factory pattern
- Runtime extensibility with per-tenant manifests
- Immutability and thread-safety in pipelines

## Code Example
```javascript
class ILogProcessor {
  async process(entry, next) { throw new Error('not implemented') }
}

class ProcessorRegistry {
  constructor(){ this.factories = new Map() }
  register(name, factory){ this.factories.set(name, factory) }
  buildPipeline(names){
    let next = async () => {}
    for (let i = names.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      const F = this.factories.get(names[i])
      const proc = new F()
      const prev = next
      next = async () => { await proc.process(null, prev) }
    }
    return next
  }
}
```

